1 @
    The @ command (the execute procedure command) runs a command
    procedure: a file of DCL commands.  The procedure runs at a new
    command level, one deeper than the level that ran it; the terminal is
    command level 0, and procedures may nest 32 levels deep.

    Syntax:

        @file-spec[/OUTPUT=file-spec] [p1 [p2 ... p8]]

    Parameters:

        file-spec       The command procedure: a file on a mounted volume
                        or on the host, by the same rules as every
                        console command's file names.  The file type
                        defaults to .COM.  A host file name with
                        lowercase letters or a "/" must be quoted.

        p1 ... p8       Up to eight parameters, separated by blanks.  The
                        procedure sees them as the local symbols P1 to
                        P8; the ones not given are "".  Letters are
                        uppercased unless quoted; quotes keep blanks and
                        case, and "" passes an empty parameter.

    Qualifier:

        /OUTPUT=file    Sends the procedure's output to the file instead
                        of the terminal (.LIS by default; NL: discards
                        it).  Error messages go to the terminal too.  A
                        file on a volume is made when the procedure
                        starts, and written a line at a time.  The
                        qualifier must follow the file name with no blank
                        between them; otherwise it is a parameter.

    In the procedure, each command line starts with "$", and "$!" starts
    a comment line.  A line ending in "-" continues on the next line,
    which has no "$".  A line without a "$" is data, for whatever reads
    the procedure's input (SYS$INPUT): an image the procedure runs (with
    LIB$GET_INPUT, RMS's $GET, or a terminal $QIO read), TYPE SYS$INPUT,
    the debugger, once a DEBUG or RUN/DEBUG in the procedure has started
    it, or the interactive assembler.  An image reading the data comes to
    its end of file at the next "$" line; DECK lets data lines start with
    "$" (see HELP DECK).  Data that nothing reads is skipped after an
    image, with a SKPDAT warning, up to the next "$" line (a lone "$"
    will do); after one of DCL's own commands (an assignment, IF, SET
    VERIFY, ...), a data line is read as a command.

    Each command level has its own local symbols (assigned with "=" or
    ":="), which go when the procedure ends; global symbols ("==" and
    ":==") stay.  A procedure sees the local symbols of the levels that
    ran it.

    The procedure ends at its last line, at an EXIT, or when a command
    fails with an error or a fatal error (a warning doesn't end it).  An
    error ends the procedures that ran it too.  ON and SET [NO]ON change
    what a failure does, and EXIT can give the procedure's status (see
    HELP ON, HELP EXIT, and HELP SYMBOL STATUS).

    Example:

        @"setup.com" "Test Run" fast

        where setup.com is:

        $! Show the parameters this procedure was given.
        $ SHOW SYMBOL P1
        $ SHOW SYMBOL P2

        shows P1 = "Test Run" and P2 = "FAST".
